      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 0,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Exercise: which poster appears in the image? (robust multi-reference detection)\n\nThree reference artworks are matched against one scene (a wall with three public-domain paintings hung at an angle), each through its own instance of the 'Feature Match + RANSAC Homography' subgraph (SIFT features, scale/rotation robust). The pipeline is failure-tolerant by design: posters that are NOT in the scene simply produce found=false and 0 inliers - nothing fails, the workflow always runs to the end. Each poster's outline is projected with its homography and drawn in its own color (poster 1 = red, poster 2 = green, poster 3 = blue). The 'found' flag drives an 'if/else' control-flow node (Basic data handling) per poster: if found, the outlined image continues down the chain; if not, the previous image passes through untouched - and because if/else has lazy inputs, the entire drawing branch of an absent poster is bypassed, never executed. The final preview shows exactly the posters that were detected - none, one, or several.\n\nSmall-descriptor handling (same problem as exercise_object_detection_homography): each high-res reference is first DOWNSCALED so its shorter edge is 200px (ComfyUI native 'Resize Images by Shorter Edge'), roughly the size a poster occupies on the wall. SIFT then describes features at scales that exist in the scene, so the references' tiny features stop producing junk matches - the resize alternative to 'Filter Keypoints'. The corners come from the RESIZED reference, so the projected outline still lands correctly. The scene side uses a high max_features (20000): 2000 spread over a wide wall would starve each small poster."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
